if you have been following along with  this series of tutorials you may have  noticed that there is no undo within  chimera other than the undo selection  feature  in this final video we will explore  three workarounds for this issue using  the hemoglobin structure for hhb  we can use the command line to save a  structure's position in the graphics  window  let's say you are ready to begin  exploring this structure but don't want  to lose the current view  the solution is to type into the command  line save pause p1 and press enter  this command defines the current  position as position 1 in chimera's  memory  now you can safely explore the structure  in the graphics window  to return to the original position all  you need to do is enter into the command  line reset p1  you can extend this basic technique to  save more than one position just label  your second position as p2 your third is  p3 and so on as you define more  positions  following the save pause command you can  also type names that are more specific  than the p plus integer naming system  that i just demonstrated in fact you can  type whatever name you want  for example we could save this position  using the command save pass  since we are focusing in on a particular  heme group  the reset format is the same as before  we just enter into the command line  reset  to recover the initial position  for this workaround it is key that you  are working within a session file that  has previously been saved using save as  chimera's file directory makes it easy  to identify such files  in this list files marked with an  extension have been saved beforehand  while those without an extension have  not  with that knowledge let's open the  hemoglobin structure we have been  looking at  the idea behind this workaround is to  save the session as you modify the  structure  let's say we go to actions ribbon and  turn on ribbon edging  now we can go to file and save the  change  then we decide to select the heme groups  and change their depiction  to sphere  but at this point you realize that you  like the heme groups better in their  original depiction so rather than saving  this change the next step is to close  the session  upon reopening it the heme groups are  restored but the ribbon edging remains  because we had previously saved that  change  in this way we can effectively create an  artificial undo within chimera  finally we can use chimera's animation  interface to store structure depiction  modifications before making any changes  let's start by taking a snapshot of the  structure in its default state  now we go to actions  color  all options and selectively color the  ribbons cyan  then we stop and take a snapshot of the  result  for our next change we will select the  heme groups  and color them red  we then take another snapshot of the  result  next we select chain a  edge its ribbons  and snapshot the result  now we have created a history of our  depiction changes within the animation  interface  by left clicking on the snapshots we can  easily recover past states of the  structure and access any of the  depictions created along the way
 

